Acknowledging the existence of ambiguity has two profound implications for nearly all annotation tasks that conforms to the premises of this study: 

• Information loss: as (Dumitrache et al., 2018a) have previously suggested, the rejection of ambiguity results in a loss of information that would impact a wide range of supervised learning applications and prevents the development of appropriate model architectures; 

• Measurement error: we find it equally concerning that if we are to generate a test set with annotation strategies that ignores ambiguity (such as one-grader, or DG,CR), we would have deliberately introduced noise into the test set by introducing ambiguous examples with an uncertain partial label. This omission would lead to increased measurement error on model performance.9
